"""
SerialTap - top-level architecture.

STUB. This file fixes the block structure and how the blocks connect; it does
NOT yet pin any parts. Every component still has to be chosen deliberately and
named by MPN (CLAUDE.md design principle 2), and two of them - the buck and the
eFuse - are gated on the power-path simulation in sim/.

Deliberately no generic passives yet: instantiating a bare Resistor or
Capacitor invites atopile's automatic part picker to resolve it to the cheapest
in-stock LCSC part, which is exactly the behaviour the project rules out.
Passives arrive with their MPNs, not before.

Architecture follows docs/SPDD.md 4.
"""

import ElectricLogic
import ElectricPower
import USB2_0


module Port:
    """
    The single level-translated UART port: a 4-pin JST XA carrying 5 V, GND, TX
    and RX. Simultaneously the data path to the target device and the board's
    primary power source (CONTEXT.md, "Port").

    TX is the pin the board drives, RX the pin it listens on - fixed, with no
    crossover jumper, because the translators are fixed-direction (ADR 0001).
    """
    power = new ElectricPower
    tx = new ElectricLogic
    rx = new ElectricLogic


module InputProtection:
    """
    Resettable fuse plus the current-limited eFuse (TPS2553 class) that bounds
    inrush by a resistor rather than by the bulk capacitance (ADR 0004).

    The limit resistor is a GATED value: sim/inrush.cir says it must sit
    strictly below the appliance's own current limit, and the appliance has not
    been measured yet.
    """
    power_in = new ElectricPower
    power_out = new ElectricPower


module SupplyOr:
    """
    Ideal-diode OR of the two supply sources, so neither can back-feed the
    other - in particular so a laptop's USB can never push current into an
    appliance's rail (FR-6).

    Ideal diodes rather than Schottkys because ~20 mV of drop keeps the rail
    genuinely at 5 V for the translators' VCCB (SPDD 5.3).
    """
    jst_in = new ElectricPower
    usb_in = new ElectricPower
    power_out = new ElectricPower


module Rail3V3:
    """
    Synchronous buck, 5 V -> 3.3 V (ADR 0003). Not an LDO: ~85 mA less peak
    draw on an appliance rail of unknown capability, and ~0.45 W less heat on a
    board with no enclosure.

    Part gated on sim/buck-load-step.cir, which needs a vendor SPICE model
    (SPDD 7.2).
    """
    power_in = new ElectricPower
    power_out = new ElectricPower


module LevelTranslator:
    """
    One fixed-direction dual-supply buffer channel. Direction is hard-wired in
    copper - no auto-direction parts (ADR 0001).

    VCCA is the 3.3 V side, VCCB the 5 V side.
    """
    power_a = new ElectricPower
    power_b = new ElectricPower
    a = new ElectricLogic
    b = new ElectricLogic


module PortProtection:
    """
    Series resistors on TX and RX plus the ESD array on the 5 V-side signals
    (SPDD 5.6). Free at 9600 baud; limits fault current if a line meets 5 V or
    GND during install.
    """
    power = new ElectricPower
    outer_tx = new ElectricLogic
    outer_rx = new ElectricLogic
    inner_tx = new ElectricLogic
    inner_rx = new ElectricLogic


module Module_ESP32C3:
    """
    ESP32-C3-MINI-1 (ADR 0002). One UART for the port; console, flashing and
    debug ride the built-in USB Serial/JTAG on GPIO18/19.
    """
    power = new ElectricPower
    uart_tx = new ElectricLogic
    uart_rx = new ElectricLogic
    usb = new USB2_0


module BenchAccess:
    """
    USB-C receptacle: the sole programming, logging and debug interface
    (ADR 0002), plus the BOOT and RESET tact switches that are the only way
    back if firmware wedges the USB peripheral.
    """
    power_out = new ElectricPower
    usb = new USB2_0


module SerialTap:
    """
    The board. See docs/SPDD.md 4 for the block diagram this mirrors.
    """
    port = new Port
    protection = new InputProtection
    supply = new SupplyOr
    rail_3v3 = new Rail3V3
    bench = new BenchAccess
    esp32 = new Module_ESP32C3
    port_protection = new PortProtection
    translate_tx = new LevelTranslator
    translate_rx = new LevelTranslator

    # --- power path (SPDD 4) ---------------------------------------------
    # JST 5 V in through the fuse and eFuse, OR-ed against USB-C VBUS.
    port.power ~ protection.power_in
    protection.power_out ~ supply.jst_in
    bench.power_out ~ supply.usb_in

    # The OR-ed 5 V rail feeds the buck and both translators' VCCB.
    supply.power_out ~ rail_3v3.power_in
    supply.power_out ~ translate_tx.power_b
    supply.power_out ~ translate_rx.power_b

    # 3.3 V feeds the module and both translators' VCCA.
    rail_3v3.power_out ~ esp32.power
    rail_3v3.power_out ~ translate_tx.power_a
    rail_3v3.power_out ~ translate_rx.power_a

    # --- signal path (SPDD 4) --------------------------------------------
    # Outward: C3 UART TX -> up-translator -> series R and ESD -> port TX.
    esp32.uart_tx ~ translate_tx.a
    translate_tx.b ~ port_protection.inner_tx
    port_protection.outer_tx ~ port.tx

    # Inward: port RX -> series R and ESD -> down-translator -> C3 UART RX.
    port.rx ~ port_protection.outer_rx
    port_protection.inner_rx ~ translate_rx.b
    translate_rx.a ~ esp32.uart_rx

    # The ESD array sits on the 5 V side, so it references that rail.
    supply.power_out ~ port_protection.power

    # --- bench access ------------------------------------------------------
    # Console, first flash and debug ride the C3's ROM USB Serial/JTAG.
    bench.usb ~ esp32.usb
